<worksheet>
  <title>Solving Quadratic equations</title>

  <!-- Optional introduction -->
  <objectives>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Using the quadratic formula to solve quadratic equations.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Using algebraic methods to solve quadratic equations.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Using the graph of a quadratic function to solve quadratic equations.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>
  <introduction>
    <p>
        The solutions to a quadratic equation <md>ax^2 + bx + c = 0</md> are the x-coordinates of the points where the graph of the function <md>f(x) = ax^2 + bx + c</md> intersects the x-axis.  In this activity, you will explore three different methods for finding these solutions.
    </p>
<theorem>
    <statement>
        <p>
            The quadratic formula states that the solutions to the equation <md>ax^2 + bx + c = 0</md> are given by:
            <md>
                x = \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}
            </md>
        </p>
    </statement>
</theorem>

  </introduction>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            In each of the following exercises, use the quadratic formula to find the solutions to the given quadratic equation.  Then check your answer by graphing the corresponding function and identifying the <m>x-</m>intercepts.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>-2(w+4)^2-8=-26</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>2x^2-3x-5=0</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>4x(4x-3)=-2</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>2(x+1)^2+12=300</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
              Find the intersepts of the graph of <m>f(x) = 2x^2-3x-5</m> with the line <m>y = 5</m>, then check your answer by graphing the function and identifying the <m>x-</m>coordinate of the intersection points.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>t^2=-4(t+1)</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>2x(x+3)-4=2x(x+1)</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <definition>
    <statement>
        <p>
           If the quadratic can be written in the factored form, that is if the graph of its parabola intersects the x-axis at  two distinct points, then the quadratic is <alert
           >factorable</alert
           >. If the parabola touches the x-axis at exactly one point, then the quadratic is a <alert>perfect square</alert>. If the parabola does not intersect the x-axis, then the quadratic is called <alert>prime</alert>
        </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        For the following exercises, use the graph of the function to determine whether the quadratic is factorable, a perfect square, or prime.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>f(x) = 2x^2-3x-5</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>f(x) = 2x^2-4x+2</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>f(x) = 2x^2-4x+5</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  </worksheet>